Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.742 --> 00:00:04.880
[Piano instrumental]

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.400
Sean Worsley, a veteran, sentenced to 5 years

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.320
in prison in Alabama for&nbsp;
using medicinal marijuana he'd

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.320
been *legally prescribed* to&nbsp;
treat a traumatic brain injury

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.440
Fair Wayne Bryant, 63 years old,

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.040
sentenced to life in prison for&nbsp;
stealing a pair of hedge clippers,

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.960
because 18 years before he'd been&nbsp;
convicted of robbing a taxi driver.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:32.160
Kalief Browder, a 16 year old held&nbsp;
in prison for 3 years in New York

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.160
for allegedly stealing a backpack.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:36.720
Charged, but never put on trial.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.400
Beaten by prison guards and&nbsp;
placed in solitary confinement

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.920
for two years, he attempted&nbsp;
suicide twice in prison.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.960
Still traumatized, he committed&nbsp;
suicide at his mother's

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.320
home after being released.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.800
[Cell closes]

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.480
These are just 3 drops in a sea of similar&nbsp;
stories all across the United States

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:58.080
Why are these stories so common?

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.760
One of the ugly truths&nbsp;
about our country is that 
 
 

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:12.160
In fact, we imprison more people&nbsp;
than any other country, period.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.560
We are the biggest prison in the world.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.360
We have a significantly larger prison population,

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:18.960
2.1 million people,

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.720
than China, a country with 3x our population

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.680
Why does the United States&nbsp;
lock up so many of its' people?

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.120
More than the entire population of New Mexico.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.440
Where did this wholesale imprisonment&nbsp;
of *HUGE* groups of people,

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:37.600
what we call mass incarceration,

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:38.100
come from?

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:40.880
Let's go back a few decades.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.440
Starting in the 1960s,

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.840
many companies, looking to cut costs,

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.400
moved production out of big cities like Detroit,

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:49.360
Milwuakee,

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:50.240
Cleveland,

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.680
or simply automated away big&nbsp;
chunks of their workforce.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.680
Their employees were left jobless,

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.800
and manufacturing was ripped out of great cities

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.360
that used to be synonymous with industry.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.920
After 1970, unemployment for&nbsp;
non-college educated men soared,

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.760
especially Black people, who were&nbsp;
just then entering the middle class.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.520
People who could leave the cities,&nbsp;
mostly middle class whites,

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.440
fled to the suburbs, a phenomenon&nbsp;
we call "white flight."

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.440
This led to a vicious spiral.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.120
Cities lost tax revenue,

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.880
schools lost funding,

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:24.400
houses lost their value,

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.800
local economies collapsed,

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.040
job opportunities dried up.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.920
As urban poverty rose, crime rose with it.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.560
And the arrival of crack-cocaine in the 80's,

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.680
helped along by U.S. foreign&nbsp;
policy in Central America,

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.840
made the problem even worse.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:45.680
By the 1970s and 80s, there was a full&nbsp;
blown social crisis in American cities

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.920
resulting in a steady increase in crime.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.800
Now, American politicians&nbsp;
*could* have responded one way

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.920
they *could* have confronted&nbsp;
the root causes of crime

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.560
by reinvesting in hollowed out cities,

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.680
building a healthier, cleaner,&nbsp;
more prosperous society

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:02.640
[music winds down]

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:03.840
But that is not what they did.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:04.640
[music changes]

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.720
Instead, they unleashed

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.320
They chose to lock up millions of people

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:15.680
rather than accept the type&nbsp;
of broad redistribution of

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.480
wealth, jobs, and power, that a&nbsp;
real solution would've demanded.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.280
Mass incarceration is expensive,

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:28.640
But for the well off, it turns out to be a&nbsp;
lot cheaper than actually solving the problem.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.280
So the U.S. government decided to combine&nbsp;
the harshest penal state in the world

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:35.520
with the stingiest welfare state.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.960
This carceral approach was&nbsp;
also effective politics,

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:44.640
with white voters terrified of rising&nbsp;
crime from increasingly non-white cities,

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.600
Republicans and Democrats alike saw a way to

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.640
exploit racist fears, and get cheap votes.

